  'Elementary Spanish I develops novice communicative ability, cultural literacy, pronunciation, and sustainable language-learning habits.',
  $esp1json${"slug": "elementary-spanish-i", "title": "Elementary Spanish I", "description": "Elementary Spanish I develops novice communicative ability, cultural literacy, pronunciation, and sustainable language-learning habits.", "topics": [{"id": "fe226346-0bbf-4101-a651-fe50edfb52a1", "type": "topic", "title": "1. Pronunciation, Spelling, and the Spanish Sound System", "description": "This opening unit trains the ear and mouth before habits become difficult to change. Students learn that Spanish spelling is more predictable than English, but still requires attention to vowels, stress, accent marks, and regional variation.", "children": [{"id": "a8d3d125-4d6a-4bfa-bccd-a67716c3d064", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Alphabet and sound-symbol correspondence", "children": [{"id": "31a314bc-4880-4df7-81e6-56752e855a75", "type": "concept", "title": "Vowel purity: a, e, i, o, u"}, {"id": "52eb8e20-a227-486b-8eca-ccb5856e5814", "type": "concept", "title": "Consonants b/v, d, g, h, j, ll/y, ñ, r/rr"}, {"id": "cb8a08b3-8614-437c-b557-9d0d172436e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Letter names for spelling aloud"}, {"id": "05f2185a-7cf5-4d74-b27a-26ccdcc8075b", "type": "concept", "title": "Sound variation across regions"}], "description": "The point is to connect letters to sounds so students can decode new words, spell what they hear, and read aloud with confidence."}, {"id": "19dbc7d1-6232-4397-9265-59442ec8f234", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Syllables, stress, and written accents", "children": [{"id": "d1fddc5f-0367-42b6-9710-c37c3b9a791d", "type": "concept", "title": "Natural stress rules"}, {"id": "5bb31e67-e96d-45de-a6d9-18c9f76bd3ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Acute accents"}, {"id": "b58a06dc-3ab5-4986-b0d3-058675f471ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Question/exclamation marks"}, {"id": "29d1320f-674f-4c09-930f-50d2f63e5ba0", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimal pairs and meaning contrasts"}], "description": "Students learn why accent marks are not decorative; they signal stress, distinguish meanings, and shape pronunciation."}, {"id": "02759c89-ebe6-4d47-8d2a-75b439ea25dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intonation and rhythm", "children": [{"id": "9e5771c4-60d9-4fa0-b5dc-40d83be0a0ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Statement/question intonation"}, {"id": "29b7e557-7d47-4bff-8083-d3c8f2646b72", "type": "concept", "title": "Linking across words"}, {"id": "44d27d45-f7a7-4c11-8aa4-0d0f651e0e02", "type": "concept", "title": "Chunking phrases"}, {"id": "44ed8bdf-3b4c-43af-84a8-99e32f70dbbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Listening for stressed syllables"}], "description": "The gist is that Spanish sounds syllable-timed and forward-moving, so learners practice phrase rhythm rather than word-by-word English intonation."}]}, {"id": "6482883b-5bcc-4d1f-bc93-dee1a16c5b4a", "type": "topic", "title": "2. Classroom Language, Greetings, and Survival Interaction", "description": "A beginner course makes Spanish usable immediately. This section gives students the language they need to participate in class, ask for help, greet people, and repair communication when they get stuck.", "children": [{"id": "50b75994-e53c-4844-a7e3-18cdeeacb36d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Greetings, farewells, and introductions", "children": [{"id": "b414417f-15f6-4d40-8b1e-309fa3f34ca7", "type": "concept", "title": "Hola, buenos días, buenas tardes"}, {"id": "4658c672-d9cf-4b1f-abc9-e7d160cc370a", "type": "concept", "title": "Me llamo..., soy de..."}, {"id": "f3a3c1c5-928b-4000-b52e-9741af74f038", "type": "concept", "title": "Mucho gusto and encantado/a"}, {"id": "db6cd877-33ce-4a3a-82b4-e417f7f9feb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal and informal address"}], "description": "Students learn social routines that make first contact possible and lower the anxiety of speaking from day one."}, {"id": "a476bc20-bee6-4e55-a005-086eafd27202", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classroom survival phrases", "children": [{"id": "5421f2ba-8e29-4303-b92b-00e50de6ef9f", "type": "concept", "title": "¿Cómo se dice...?"}, {"id": "741ff67a-12b5-46c3-a3db-e958a167e5f2", "type": "concept", "title": "No entiendo"}, {"id": "619c9708-3d13-4a80-a9c7-01d7f7c9dde3", "type": "concept", "title": "Repita, por favor"}, {"id": "69b8d29e-0dfa-4ece-9325-1088fb27d750", "type": "concept", "title": "¿Qué significa...?"}], "description": "The point is functional independence: learners can ask for repetition, clarification, permission, and vocabulary without switching entirely to English."}, {"id": "0c99b845-0736-4f00-9870-cf950e824433", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Politeness and repair strategies", "children": [{"id": "1e84ad58-7353-4c02-8f3f-f6034c32f573", "type": "concept", "title": "Por favor and gracias"}, {"id": "28830f97-3268-4f12-853e-5fc537823641", "type": "concept", "title": "Perdón and disculpe"}, {"id": "f7d55045-30a6-48ae-878f-e5a7da1c57f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Slower speech requests"}, {"id": "f9d01063-11b7-4bc6-8814-50cc09e5aba5", "type": "concept", "title": "Gesture and circumlocution"}], "description": "Students learn that communication includes managing breakdowns gracefully, not producing perfect sentences."}]}, {"id": "cbcc91f1-75c1-4a73-ac88-8f1d44c729ae", "type": "topic", "title": "3. Subject Pronouns and Present-Tense Regular Verbs", "description": "This unit gives students a productive engine for saying what people do. It also introduces a key difference from English: Spanish verb endings often identify the subject without an explicit pronoun.", "children": [{"id": "81ec4caa-d656-4fca-97d8-84575977d222", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Subject pronouns and person/number", "children": [{"id": "f225295d-60b5-4c49-9536-8078ecaf5db7", "type": "concept", "title": "yo, tú, usted"}, {"id": "0523a1b7-8911-4ec3-917c-3af20435452d", "type": "concept", "title": "él, ella"}, {"id": "9bdfe138-bf2c-4d82-b0f5-7a33343ffc0e", "type": "concept", "title": "nosotros/as"}, {"id": "79cbdf90-03aa-4d22-8c41-46118badbbe5", "type": "concept", "title": "ellos/as, ustedes"}], "description": "Students learn pronouns as a map for conjugation, while also seeing why Spanish often drops them."}, {"id": "7f0b7c20-57b5-407a-93f5-86fd23d59044", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regular -ar verbs", "children": [{"id": "1ac55bee-c243-4a4a-87cf-0fa62a9d2acf", "type": "concept", "title": "hablar, estudiar, trabajar"}, {"id": "5e865f14-a1f4-4f22-a936-ba27a39de7f2", "type": "concept", "title": "stem plus endings"}, {"id": "7aa77cda-7851-40db-83ae-a354c36c9675", "type": "concept", "title": "yo form and agreement"}, {"id": "b8fe6055-db6e-4bc6-adc5-0f6f3a661701", "type": "concept", "title": "frequency expressions"}], "description": "The first regular pattern lets students speak about common routines with high payoff."}, {"id": "abfd8dbd-65d0-4a89-a372-9cade42097a3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regular -er and -ir verbs", "children": [{"id": "8c532da7-8f3c-42b8-99ef-c4f113cefa66", "type": "concept", "title": "comer, beber, leer"}, {"id": "10fb4956-7f8c-4ab0-a3b0-2d565e420311", "type": "concept", "title": "vivir, escribir, asistir"}, {"id": "6c06f7f6-e4d1-4324-b833-5ac407abed3a", "type": "concept", "title": "negative forms"}, {"id": "9dfb86fd-3c01-470f-8e12-1ba93609c96b", "type": "concept", "title": "habitual present meaning"}], "description": "Students extend the same logic to more verb families, noticing similarities and differences across patterns."}]}, {"id": "9d667517-fccf-4571-8416-38aaccd6b914", "type": "topic", "title": "4. Gender, Number, Articles, and Noun-Adjective Agreement", "description": "Spanish meaning depends on agreement. This section trains students to hear and produce the small endings that make nouns, articles, and adjectives work together.", "children": [{"id": "fcfc0f1e-21e9-427e-adce-333824801474", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Grammatical gender", "children": [{"id": "ee9a621a-0fc7-4bbd-8a8e-1f38faabb2d1", "type": "concept", "title": "masculine -o patterns"}, {"id": "8cc7a839-fe74-4f83-9df0-4064237d924c", "type": "concept", "title": "feminine -a patterns"}, {"id": "c5119498-8d0d-4c5d-b99b-b5417b976ea4", "type": "concept", "title": "common exceptions"}, {"id": "6491d7e7-1e00-48f2-984b-3d34aca34a53", "type": "concept", "title": "people nouns and inclusive awareness"}], "description": "Students learn gender as a property of nouns, not a statement about real-world identity, and begin recognizing common patterns and exceptions."}, {"id": "e2caa400-04b0-473b-8952-c70dc9b2ea15", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Singular and plural formation", "children": [{"id": "74fc40ac-fcf7-4f88-8f07-5aac78a34dbe", "type": "concept", "title": "adding -s or -es"}, {"id": "9e5442a5-7dac-4829-82ae-70003905ddde", "type": "concept", "title": "words ending in consonants"}, {"id": "74599c56-19b5-4855-9eaa-d3fba6e74772", "type": "concept", "title": "plural articles"}, {"id": "9cc6f24f-a21c-4192-abc0-06e3df70679c", "type": "concept", "title": "agreement across a phrase"}], "description": "The goal is to make quantity visible and audible through articles, nouns, adjectives, and verbs."}, {"id": "f59b6dbb-38ee-433d-b2c9-48eeb3931765", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Articles and adjective placement", "children": [{"id": "c19c4a73-9576-4e49-b3f7-9208f09e9cd3", "type": "concept", "title": "el, la, los, las"}, {"id": "807477e5-38e2-40d2-8869-466548f98d70", "type": "concept", "title": "un, una, unos, unas"}, {"id": "2808ea12-46b7-41db-9f6b-92ec43259d07", "type": "concept", "title": "descriptive adjectives"}, {"id": "406278d7-0922-45e1-a523-3afaab1f6880", "type": "concept", "title": "meaning-changing adjective position"}], "description": "Students learn how definite and indefinite articles shape meaning and how adjectives normally follow nouns in Spanish."}]}, {"id": "1499b78b-77b8-49f7-baf2-725e658cdd6f", "type": "topic", "title": "5. Ser, Estar, Hay, Tener, and Ir", "description": "These high-frequency verbs let beginners say much more than regular verb patterns alone. Students use them to identify, locate, describe states, express possession, talk about obligations, and make near-future plans.", "children": [{"id": "2e594d2d-86e6-46a1-baaa-1cc0e446f83a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ser for identity and inherent description", "children": [{"id": "cf93b81a-daec-44cd-be83-0983fcfcb138", "type": "concept", "title": "soy, eres, es"}, {"id": "f25ea136-bd04-42e0-a1d6-9133d6b5c849", "type": "concept", "title": "origin with de"}, {"id": "cef7f9ee-96a6-41f7-9982-59cbe509cf94", "type": "concept", "title": "time and date"}, {"id": "a33f0b3f-2106-40eb-be0d-46524135e168", "type": "concept", "title": "identity and characteristics"}], "description": "Students use ser for names, origins, professions, personality, time, and stable descriptions."}, {"id": "2b78fc21-1fc4-4680-b9c1-621eceb3508b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Estar and hay for states, location, and existence", "children": [{"id": "69db7eb5-0c45-40dc-af69-42392e25942c", "type": "concept", "title": "estoy, estás, está"}, {"id": "1ee70432-ecf3-47c8-9193-7716eacd0760", "type": "concept", "title": "location with prepositions"}, {"id": "c244a6d4-3e05-4eed-aac7-9e1167be68da", "type": "concept", "title": "hay for there is/are"}, {"id": "80c2e9b4-3757-42dc-b948-8e5aa73f67bc", "type": "concept", "title": "temporary conditions"}], "description": "The point is to distinguish where something is, how someone feels, and whether something exists."}, {"id": "fd5fa559-08fa-4cda-b595-5bfbd74847f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tener, tener que, ir, and ir a + infinitive", "children": [{"id": "1c7e9d60-08f7-413a-a692-b74ef449a26b", "type": "concept", "title": "tener expressions"}, {"id": "7947df62-6799-49fe-b811-33c166da8608", "type": "concept", "title": "age and possession"}, {"id": "f9400c78-25ca-40f7-ac25-c5d82b526326", "type": "concept", "title": "ir + a + place"}, {"id": "730d06cc-103d-4b6c-9a8b-4a79404b59e5", "type": "concept", "title": "near future: ir a + infinitive"}], "description": "These verbs unlock practical beginner communication about age, possession, obligations, destinations, and immediate plans."}]}, {"id": "cfd327b6-9521-487d-9051-a7a579516ab9", "type": "topic", "title": "6. Basic Questions, Negation, and Word Order", "description": "Conversation depends on asking, answering, and clarifying. This section teaches students how Spanish forms questions and negatives, and how word order shifts with communicative purpose.", "children": [{"id": "6b1b10e5-1776-431b-b2be-4d4855b2a61c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Yes/no questions", "children": [{"id": "3f0e35ed-bfc1-4fc1-b536-69d04377851b", "type": "concept", "title": "inverted question marks"}, {"id": "8e3aaaae-e32c-4cce-816d-97d6d029a65f", "type": "concept", "title": "rising intonation"}, {"id": "297f0ee9-3256-4811-a4b8-c77d4c1581cf", "type": "concept", "title": "subject placement"}, {"id": "12e1410a-e653-4303-845e-a7f4a2323daf", "type": "concept", "title": "tag-style follow-up questions"}], "description": "Students learn that intonation and punctuation can signal a question even when the word order looks familiar."}, {"id": "fd91889e-9378-4533-b56a-594261cd5b59", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Question words", "children": [{"id": "98c7f02a-8afe-409a-bc4f-34b9adc1e3bb", "type": "concept", "title": "qué, quién, cuándo"}, {"id": "f3dded97-3017-4194-bb44-3888be1c1017", "type": "concept", "title": "dónde, de dónde, adónde"}, {"id": "b1e9acfd-7a02-4b0c-aa05-d221d88e3261", "type": "concept", "title": "por qué and porque"}, {"id": "dcda35eb-16d4-4642-8fad-16110a648f2c", "type": "concept", "title": "cuánto/a/os/as"}], "description": "The goal is to gather real information: who, what, when, where, why, how, and how much."}, {"id": "3ee15cea-bf37-49a7-973d-a14314b80dbe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Negation and sentence rhythm", "children": [{"id": "268aae23-63b0-4157-a786-1689b38afd3e", "type": "concept", "title": "no before the verb"}, {"id": "47274fdf-f037-4c38-93bc-af003c46f50d", "type": "concept", "title": "nunca and tampoco"}, {"id": "5fb1a131-b5d5-46ea-a1ea-c36086cb9c2e", "type": "concept", "title": "negative answers"}, {"id": "e5d6594c-d09f-4909-99c0-60ee541bbab9", "type": "concept", "title": "word order in simple statements"}], "description": "Students learn to make accurate negative statements and avoid English-based word order habits."}]}, {"id": "21c58f75-f6be-4875-bbd2-ec4312aa21ea", "type": "topic", "title": "7. Everyday Vocabulary, Descriptions, Preferences, and Possession", "description": "Vocabulary becomes durable when it is attached to personal meaning. This unit helps students describe their world: people, courses, family, places, likes, belongings, and daily activities.", "children": [{"id": "7a053912-bf03-457f-9f21-df350a62f8f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "People, family, and communities", "children": [{"id": "4034e865-f6f6-40f9-af21-fa4af8f76e8e", "type": "concept", "title": "family vocabulary"}, {"id": "ef896cc3-ca1f-4a5f-8233-0cecded621ac", "type": "concept", "title": "chosen community language"}, {"id": "00e3ff7f-2f10-4a06-9de7-7d9546308d80", "type": "concept", "title": "physical and personality adjectives"}, {"id": "273e8e1c-3038-437f-935f-2be41a9b9481", "type": "concept", "title": "possessive adjectives"}], "description": "Students learn to describe relationships in culturally aware ways while practicing agreement and possession."}, {"id": "c12722c2-6999-45ed-9002-c512d9843a12", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Academic and daily-life vocabulary", "children": [{"id": "092b0187-730d-4687-8610-757d5d288d24", "type": "concept", "title": "courses and majors"}, {"id": "9a1488e0-985f-4f89-82b8-572e30db122c", "type": "concept", "title": "classroom objects"}, {"id": "370acd97-6356-49e0-9fb7-90aa0318f4c9", "type": "concept", "title": "places on campus"}, {"id": "a6a85833-1400-4dea-89bf-dd72a3321832", "type": "concept", "title": "daily activities"}], "description": "The point is to make Spanish relevant to students' actual routines and college environment."}, {"id": "401c9891-642f-4b38-b303-76d3ee573098", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gustar and preferences", "children": [{"id": "db88c041-231c-45f6-a3f5-fd0dfec8533b", "type": "concept", "title": "me gusta / me gustan"}, {"id": "65d0aee0-2083-4d93-bd79-b00453ca1d6c", "type": "concept", "title": "te gusta and le gusta"}, {"id": "f94ddfb0-51dc-42e9-a355-73a04efd0274", "type": "concept", "title": "infinitives after gustar"}, {"id": "f5962487-4454-4c63-866f-701f700c0195", "type": "concept", "title": "also/neither: también/tampoco"}], "description": "Students learn a high-frequency structure that works differently from English and is essential for real conversation."}]}, {"id": "39d3b094-8390-4d47-a1bb-dd2f29877fe0", "type": "topic", "title": "8. Numbers, Time, Calendar, Schedules, and Quantity", "description": "This section makes Spanish practical for daily logistics. Students use numbers and time expressions to coordinate meetings, talk about ages and dates, read schedules, and handle prices or quantities.", "children": [{"id": "efcceaf2-dd3d-459d-a63b-ad03981cf71d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Numbers and basic quantities", "children": [{"id": "f25566ae-48b2-4df8-b9cc-811e788a9b78", "type": "concept", "title": "0-100 and beyond"}, {"id": "5bcbf0bb-fc67-4665-990d-7eb1acb2912a", "type": "concept", "title": "phone numbers"}, {"id": "1208a551-196e-433d-9b39-83488b879feb", "type": "concept", "title": "prices"}, {"id": "57effea0-2a82-4b93-a5a4-6be71411f4ae", "type": "concept", "title": "agreement with uno/una"}], "description": "Students learn numbers as communicative tools, not isolated memorization."}, {"id": "29337658-fc31-472a-bef6-226523bf6d97", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Clock time and schedules", "children": [{"id": "fb55af08-5939-4cf7-b16c-36ec86917042", "type": "concept", "title": "¿Qué hora es?"}, {"id": "7c718132-7e5a-4e1f-acb5-82ffbaebe5ec", "type": "concept", "title": "a la una / a las dos"}, {"id": "e7000c80-8aa1-499d-a731-fb089babbdda", "type": "concept", "title": "de...a..."}, {"id": "31d33057-4f06-4d57-94c8-7330ecd05f23", "type": "concept", "title": "morning, afternoon, evening"}], "description": "The point is to talk about when things happen and understand common schedule language."}, {"id": "9fc7274c-c2df-4f2a-a1dc-df0244293a92", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dates and calendar language", "children": [{"id": "f1f6c712-5c5e-4381-8e8e-5e1835fbe8cb", "type": "concept", "title": "days and months"}, {"id": "a74504c7-5ca4-4ee2-95e1-1558e38500f4", "type": "concept", "title": "birthdays"}, {"id": "fcceb1e3-0e77-41ad-87e2-a79b89543733", "type": "concept", "title": "seasons"}, {"id": "44bf3faa-893c-4e5f-8ec9-19741a798ce1", "type": "concept", "title": "class and event planning"}], "description": "Students learn to situate events across the week, month, semester, and year."}]}, {"id": "695af733-93d0-431e-bddf-21e1c6414947", "type": "topic", "title": "9. Listening, Speaking, and Task-Based Communication", "description": "Grammar knowledge only matters when students can use it under real-time conditions. This unit turns structures into novice performance through input, interaction, repetition, and purposeful tasks.", "children": [{"id": "180bbad9-2613-417c-9b25-e04e09238a5c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comprehensible input", "children": [{"id": "5ebe1559-9248-4312-9a30-8bf96c5a65fb", "type": "concept", "title": "gist listening"}, {"id": "0b6028fc-b82d-49ca-9cae-5fe6b8240bbb", "type": "concept", "title": "specific-detail listening"}, {"id": "f94f7206-d312-4b07-96e2-a310cbb7ac5a", "type": "concept", "title": "cognates and context clues"}, {"id": "985b9f04-2a88-423e-b324-fa00f3e58f7b", "type": "concept", "title": "tolerance for ambiguity"}], "description": "Students train listening by using context, visuals, cognates, repetition, and prediction rather than translating every word."}, {"id": "59fc3270-c19e-496f-8418-ed4ebdf66ecd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpersonal speaking tasks", "children": [{"id": "13eb3bf1-5631-4158-a811-cd451788637f", "type": "concept", "title": "information gaps"}, {"id": "d9fc3043-dc7b-44af-a1eb-3efbb27989e1", "type": "concept", "title": "interviews"}, {"id": "977b53b2-2263-4fb6-9dc5-65ac0ea827dd", "type": "concept", "title": "role plays"}, {"id": "ed41188c-2e5f-486f-9fa5-ad60ae49a9e1", "type": "concept", "title": "follow-up questions"}], "description": "The goal is to exchange information with another person, even with limited language."}, {"id": "410ac5a7-caa9-46c4-9067-b0b89152828f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Presentational speaking", "children": [{"id": "647682a8-b9c7-44e1-a66a-f2ba2920421f", "type": "concept", "title": "short self-introductions"}, {"id": "13d29f9b-9a9d-4a9b-bd18-1e66a1a72b74", "type": "concept", "title": "campus or family descriptions"}, {"id": "76001efc-d7a7-46dd-ae81-d7bcb174d7f1", "type": "concept", "title": "preference reports"}, {"id": "30de9779-8ad9-4b7a-9036-13aa964214a6", "type": "concept", "title": "pronunciation self-checks"}], "description": "Students learn to prepare short spoken messages that are accurate, comprehensible, and personally meaningful."}]}, {"id": "753b64ba-d6c3-473a-9433-38860509ae78", "type": "topic", "title": "10. Short Guided Writing and Sentence-Building", "description": "Writing gives beginners time to notice accuracy. Students learn to build sentences, combine them into short paragraphs, revise for agreement and verb forms, and use simple connectors.", "children": [{"id": "4b6d21d1-b795-40ad-96e6-0cccfa3a8362", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sentence construction", "children": [{"id": "d0d6df4a-4378-4267-a8c7-86fa6720f3bc", "type": "concept", "title": "subject-verb agreement"}, {"id": "cd22a141-e34a-4c94-b148-9ae73b49a660", "type": "concept", "title": "noun-adjective agreement"}, {"id": "297e669f-5f73-483f-823f-f97daf96e528", "type": "concept", "title": "punctuation and accents"}, {"id": "a7256692-941d-4d7f-bb84-8a363bd87146", "type": "concept", "title": "basic word order"}], "description": "Students practice building complete, accurate sentences before moving into paragraphs."}, {"id": "b51c2395-7529-47ed-962a-aa82b816a95a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Paragraph organization", "children": [{"id": "ecbbcf1c-fa46-49f1-9abe-b76a7b93b793", "type": "concept", "title": "topic sentence"}, {"id": "7160eb3f-b49e-45d1-b126-72c288bdbc33", "type": "concept", "title": "supporting details"}, {"id": "00dae471-448c-436e-976f-7393d818c6d4", "type": "concept", "title": "connectors: y, pero, también, porque"}, {"id": "9bfa7a67-f75a-406a-bd0b-63680df7150f", "type": "concept", "title": "logical sequence"}], "description": "The point is to move from isolated sentences to a small coherent message."}, {"id": "5bb4efcf-fe50-4b7c-b4db-a3a2c9214216", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revision and self-correction", "children": [{"id": "6c1703c1-c07e-4229-98f6-2856fafc1c0d", "type": "concept", "title": "verb ending check"}, {"id": "8c5ffbcd-db28-4e97-b145-b5cc2861f339", "type": "concept", "title": "gender/number check"}, {"id": "b9789fc0-8f5e-418a-96f9-88eeed4ade6c", "type": "concept", "title": "accent mark check"}, {"id": "4bcd1df0-230b-4f9b-b601-233a66d8ffdb", "type": "concept", "title": "peer feedback"}], "description": "Students learn that accuracy improves through habits of checking, not just teacher correction."}]}, {"id": "8c80748c-223b-4d42-b1c1-e3905075f5f3", "type": "topic", "title": "11. Cultural Introductions and Intercultural Comparison", "description": "Culture is not an add-on after grammar. Students encounter Spanish as a language used across diverse communities, histories, identities, registers, and daily practices.", "children": [{"id": "1c3c5dac-16a9-4471-bfb9-4e6e8250fa54", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Geography and diversity of the Spanish-speaking world", "children": [{"id": "4152371c-b5df-48f1-b412-4846018704ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Spain and Latin America"}, {"id": "54b5025c-7f04-4d11-aa3a-1eff526d3db7", "type": "concept", "title": "U.S. Spanish-speaking communities"}, {"id": "1f8da675-e5d8-4b49-9124-d40a255e40f3", "type": "concept", "title": "regional vocabulary"}, {"id": "eb7bd1b6-d224-4622-986c-e3089789ffa3", "type": "concept", "title": "language contact"}], "description": "Students begin replacing a single-story view of Spanish with a map of varied regions and communities."}, {"id": "7f023f19-71fc-4dd8-814c-dcc50aadcf73", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Products, practices, and perspectives", "children": [{"id": "fcb63d58-14b8-4702-9c60-6a2130a921f7", "type": "concept", "title": "names and naming practices"}, {"id": "e6e327d3-fcb6-4175-8589-a2201551d9e5", "type": "concept", "title": "mealtimes and greetings"}, {"id": "66cea9ad-06f9-4f09-a9af-97109597c156", "type": "concept", "title": "education and family contexts"}, {"id": "9b90f23c-1a41-4dee-b301-e81e2a38cf69", "type": "concept", "title": "music, media, and public life"}], "description": "The goal is to connect visible cultural items and routines to deeper values and social meanings."}, {"id": "ca228e19-e467-47f8-bca4-ceb20c4d548c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intercultural reflection", "children": [{"id": "5a73172f-dd8c-4025-aec0-7eec5e970b02", "type": "concept", "title": "similarities and differences"}, {"id": "a01dd2f0-798b-49fc-9755-adf6f4931182", "type": "concept", "title": "register and politeness"}, {"id": "d74fac4a-2cd5-4037-96bd-f35f931791cb", "type": "concept", "title": "avoidance of overgeneralization"}, {"id": "1367d551-b9c9-4443-8145-660f71c63b64", "type": "concept", "title": "respectful curiosity"}], "description": "Students learn to compare without stereotyping and to ask better questions about difference."}]}, {"id": "70f44b81-d785-4e00-bf1c-0bbf5de66146", "type": "topic", "title": "12. Integrated Novice Portfolio and Independent Learning Strategies", "description": "The final unit consolidates the semester and prepares students for Elementary Spanish II. Students collect evidence of what they can do and learn how to keep improving outside class.", "children": [{"id": "9d35cc3e-f783-4ef9-847f-2d17a0c30c8e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Novice portfolio", "children": [{"id": "c82115fc-1afc-45cf-8fdf-442755885fe2", "type": "concept", "title": "oral sample"}, {"id": "22ab4709-79d0-41af-af0c-238a98a38ceb", "type": "concept", "title": "revised writing"}, {"id": "2d9de134-f931-40c1-88f8-1a9e7a0cbb03", "type": "concept", "title": "listening log"}, {"id": "e7502232-4650-4acc-836f-508caea5640e", "type": "concept", "title": "cultural reflection"}], "description": "Students demonstrate growth across speaking, listening, reading, writing, vocabulary, grammar, and culture."}, {"id": "fa004109-9a6b-4765-b9f2-ecf3bdf046fb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vocabulary and grammar study strategies", "children": [{"id": "29703272-ae21-4059-a6bc-dfd76c0d13f9", "type": "concept", "title": "flashcard design"}, {"id": "78ddbc49-9baf-4619-b5d8-0e535c62d8b2", "type": "concept", "title": "retrieval practice"}, {"id": "f187ded2-83a6-41ea-acd0-be66b3191a3d", "type": "concept", "title": "verb charts with meaning"}, {"id": "2e79e9f9-6c9d-4ec3-aae5-c352c69cab51", "type": "concept", "title": "error log"}], "description": "The point is to help students study smarter, with retrieval, spaced repetition, and patterns rather than passive rereading."}, {"id": "074525c0-e8c4-48ff-bb21-7dbbb2d8ca67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Independent input and pronunciation habits", "children": [{"id": "ec58879e-06b5-41fd-955e-3ac26998a4c0", "type": "concept", "title": "graded videos"}, {"id": "1d81f595-4904-4d6f-a7e8-933c42694ac7", "type": "concept", "title": "short daily listening"}, {"id": "ae9350eb-3038-46bb-88cc-133e1b528c70", "type": "concept", "title": "read-aloud practice"}, {"id": "f89f5816-d331-4726-be6f-7dfc5d2cf158", "type": "concept", "title": "self-assessment against novice goals"}], "description": "Students learn sustainable routines for listening, shadowing, reading, and self-recording."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$esp1json$::jsonb,
